        "math_academy_id": "q-129143",
        "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}[f(t)-\\frac{3}{2}g(t)]\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e8\\frac{1}{2},\\,6,\\,-1\u27e9$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e81,\\,-2,\\,-6\u27e9.$",
        "worked_solution": "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[f(t)-\\frac{3}{2}g(t)]\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[f(t)-\\frac{3}{2}g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2},\\,6,\\,-1\u27e9-\\frac{3}{2}\u27e81,\\,-2,\\,-6\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2},\\,6,\\,-1\u27e9-\u27e8\\frac{3}{2}\\,-3,\\,-9\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,\\,9,\\,8\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
